      "id": 1,
      "text": "Columbia Cross Roads began as a crossroads settlement; its post office opened in 1818 as Sylvania, reflecting early agricultural trade.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        19,
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/columbia_cross_roads_pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The first log cabin appeared in 1795, built by Richard Doty, marking the township’s earliest permanent dwelling.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.bradfordcountypa.gov/208/Columbia-Township"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1813 Columbia Township was officially formed from parts of Lycoming County, following land disputes between Pennsylvania and Connecticut.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15,
        4,
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.bradfordcountypa.gov/208/Columbia-Township"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The 1898 arrival of the Northern Central Railway brought a train station to Columbia Cross Roads, linking it to Troy and Sayre.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/columbia_cross_roads_pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Columbia Cross Roads hosts a family farmer’s market from spring to mid‑November, showcasing produce from nearby Amish farms.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
